sed. We have our own notes: D responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as moody, loner, hot temper, know-it-all. So D has responsibility, concentration, problem solving, but moody, loner, hot temper, know-it-all => crew cooperation issues, stress resilience questionable.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate is best overall. Let's evaluate each with all criteria:\n\nCandidate A: strengths: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Concerns: unfriendly (show-off), not open to new ideas, unorganized, not good at taking criticism, eats unhealthily (maybe irrelevant). So reliability? Leadership good, but unorganized and not open to criticism may affect reliability and adaptability. Stress handling unknown. Technical competence strong. Decision quality unknown. Attention unknown. Crew cooperation concerns (unfriendly, show-off). Professional communication concerns (unfriendly). Responsibility? Leadership suggests responsibility, but unorganized may affect. Adaptability concerns (not open to new ideas). Overall mixed.\n\nCandidate B: strengths: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical). Concerns: grumpy/uncooperative, makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation, communication), weak memory for numbers (attention/accuracy). Also regarded as pretentious. So reliability strong, stress strong, technical strong, but crew cooperation issues, attention issues.\n\nCandidate C: strengths: can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality), keeps calm in crisis (stress resilience), excellent attention skills, puts concern for others above everything (crew cooperation positive), understands complicated technology (technical). Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas (communication), regarded as egocentric (crew cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So overall strong in many criteria, but communication and egocentric may affect crew cooperation. However, \"puts concern for others above everything\" suggests positive crew cooperation, but difficulty communicating may hinder. Mixed.\n\nCandidate D: strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately (adaptability), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical/problem solving), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: hot temper (stress resilience, crew cooperation), moody, loner, know-it-all (crew cooperation, communication). So strong technical and responsibility, but interpersonal issues.\n\nNow we need to decide which candidate best fits overall. Candidate C seems strong in decision quality, stress, attention, technical, and has concern for others, but communication difficulty and egocentric may be mitigated? Egocentric suggests self-centered, could be problematic. But \"puts concern for others above everything\" suggests altruism. This is contradictory. But we have both: concern for others, but egocentric. Could be ambiguous.\n\nCandidate B has strong reliability and stress, but crew cooperation issues and attention numeric memory. Numeric memory may be important for flight data. Could be a safety risk.\n\nCandidate A has strong anticipation and leadership, but unorganized and not open to criticism, which could be safety risk.\n\nCandidate D has responsibility and problem solving, but hot temper and moody, which could affect stress and crew.\n\nGiven the safety-critical nature, crew cooperation and communication are essential. Candidate C's difficulty communicating is a serious issue. Candidate B's nasty remarks also serious. Candidate D's hot temper also serious. Candidate A's unfriendly but maybe less severe? Unorganized is also serious.\n\nWe need to weigh. Perhaps Candidate A's unorganized nature is a bigger risk than B's interpersonal remarks? But A's lack of openness to criticism could hinder learning. B's grumpy may affect crew but can be trained. B's weak numeric memory could be mitigated with checklists.\n\nCandidate C's communication difficulty may be a bigger safety risk because clear communication is vital. So maybe C is not best.\n\nThus likely best is between A and B. Which one? A's technical anticipation and leadership are valuable for long-distance flights. B's reliability and stress handling are also valuable. But A's unorganized nature could affect flight planning. B's interpersonal issues could affect cockpit environment. Which is more critical? Probably reliability and stress are core, but crew cooperation is also essential. Both have issues.\n\nWe could argue that B's conscientiousness and stress handling outweigh his interpersonal issues, which can be mitigated with training, while A's unorganized nature is more fundamental to safe operations. So we might vote for B.\n\nThus as Agent 3, we can state that after hearing about C's stress handling and crew cooperation, we see that C's communication issues remain a concern, and D also has crew issues. Between A and B, B's reliability and stress handling are stronger, and his interpersonal issues are less safety-critical than A's organizational problems. Therefore I recommend Candidate B.\n\nWe need to reference facts: B very conscientious, handles stress well, but grumpy/uncooperative. A unorganized, not good at taking criticism. Also mention C's stress handling good but crew cooperation poor. D's hot temper.\n\nThus final vote: Candidate B.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON."}
